# Runtime Safety And Integrations

Use this rule when changing runtime entrypoints, env parsing, background jobs,
or outbound notifications.

## Core Pattern

- Parse boolean env vars explicitly. Never use `z.coerce.boolean()` for
  string-backed env values because `"false"` becomes `true`.
- Service entrypoints must both wire routes and start listening. A bootstrap
  log without a server is not enough.
- Use `pg-boss` job APIs for queue smoke checks: `createQueue()`, `send()`,
  and `work()` handlers that receive job arrays.
- External sends must have dry-run logging, timeout, bounded retry, and
  non-2xx logging.
- No module-level side effects in shared packages. Do not call `readEnv()`,
  open DB pools, or instantiate clients at import time. Use a lazy factory
  (e.g. `getPool()`) so imports stay test-safe and fork-safe.
- `node-pg-migrate` `-d` is `--database-url-var` and takes an env-var
  **name**, not the expanded URL. Rely on the default `DATABASE_URL`
  lookup or pass `-d DATABASE_URL` explicitly.

## Integration Points

- `packages/config/src/env.ts` owns env parsing contracts.
- `apps/service/src/index.ts` owns HTTP startup.
- `scripts/run-provider-smoke.ts` owns the pg-boss publish/consume smoke.
- `packages/notifications/src/telegram/telegram-notifier.ts` owns Telegram
  delivery behavior.
- `packages/persistence/src/db.ts` exposes the lazy `getPool()` factory.
- `package.json` `migrate:up` / `migrate:down` show the correct
  `node-pg-migrate` invocation.

## Common Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Preferred handling |
| --- | --- |
| Treating `"false"` as false via coercion | Use explicit `"true"`/`"false"` parsing |
| Logging "bootstrapped" without listening | Verify `/health` responds locally |
| Using `publish()` to enqueue pg-boss jobs | Use `send()` after `createQueue()` |
| Swallowing dry-run alerts | Log the rendered payload |
| Eager `new Pool()` or `readEnv()` at import | Use a lazy `getPool()` factory |
| `node-pg-migrate -d $DATABASE_URL` | Rely on default lookup or `-d DATABASE_URL` |

